{
	"_comment": "Cargo & Ore industry (scripts/sim/industry.gd): every good the type trades has its building. Mines dig ore and logging camps fell timber at their resource ports, on claims shared by every company (claims: how many a port has; a company may hold most_claims of a port's); plants refine ore into metals and other goods for the breakbulk carriers; factories make the container ships' goods. Plants and factories go at any port the company has unlocked, one of each kind a port. A building makes output_a_month[kind] of its good (in its unit) into its stockpile (holding stock_months of it), from inputs (units of each input good a unit of output) out of its input store: what its company's ships unload there (up to stock_months of what it uses), else bought at the port's price as it needs them (so a building its own ships supply earns more). Its ships load its stockpile free; it can be sold there too. Cost: the building's monthly output value over its inputs at base prices x cost_months, rounded to the nearest $1M and kept within cost_range (no_input_factor more for a factory or plant that takes nothing shipped), unless the building sets its own cost; upkeep upkeep_a_month of it a calendar month, paid a day at a time. Closing one, close_share comes back. goods: the goods added for the type (GameData adds them to the markets at load, as Manufacturers' are): line is the ships that carry them (container, ore, breakbulk), regions their region prices (x base) over the line's default_regions, sources the ports where they're cheapest (a mine's or camp's ports are added at mine_price). The container ships' and ore carriers' new goods are priced so each line's average base stays as it was (the XP weight behind the level costs).",
